| [[Abstract strategy game|Abstract strategy]]
| An abstract strategy game is a game where player decisions, rather than random elements, determine the outcome. These games are [[Combinatorial game theory|combinatorial]], meaning they provide [[perfect information]] rather than hidden or imperfect information, and rely on neither [[Fine motor skill|physical dexterity]] nor non-deterministic elements like [[Shuffling|shuffled cards]] or dice rolls during gameplay. Nearly all abstract strategy games are designed for two players or teams taking a finite number of alternating turns. Most games in this category are themeless or have minimal themes and typically lack narrative elements.
| [[List of abstract strategy games|Examples of abstract strategy games]] that are also board games include:<ul>Classical: [[Chess]]; [[Checkers]]; [[Go (board game)|Go]]; [[Reversi]]; [[Tafl games]]{{pb}} Modern: ''[[Abalone (game)|Abalone]]''; ''[[Arimaa]]''; ''[[Blokus]]''; ''[[Dameo]]''; All games in the ''[[GIPF Project]]'' series; ''[[Hive (game)|Hive]]''; ''[[Onitama]]''; ''[[Pylos (board game)|Pylos]]''; ''[[Quarto (board game)|Quarto]]''; ''[[Quoridor]]''; ''[[Santorini (game)|Santorini]]''; ''[[Stratego]]''; ''[[Tak (game)|Tak]]''; ''[[Tigris and Euphrates (game)|Tigris and Euphrates]]''; ''[[YINSH]]'' </ul>
